= Occam's razor — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''Occam's razor''' (also written '''Ockham's razor''') is a principle of reasoning that advises us not to add assumptions, entities or complications without a good reason. When two explanations account for the available evidence equally well, the less complicated one may be a better starting point. But the simplest explanation is '''not''' guaranteed to be true, and competing explanations are rarely equally well supported in every respect.<ref name="sep">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/simplicity/ ''Simplicity''], philosophical forms, justifications and qualifications of the principle.</ref></onlyinclude>

== A practical example ==

Suppose an electronic sensor suddenly reports an unusual value. One explanation is that a connector became loose. Another involves a complicated chain of unlikely failures.

Checking the connector first may be sensible because it is easy to test and does not require several unsupported assumptions. But if measurements show that the connector is secure, the simple explanation should lose support. No simplicity rule allows an investigator to ignore evidence.

This is a hypothetical example, not a claim about how often particular sensor faults occur.

== What counts as simple? ==

Different people can mean different things:

* Fewer unsupported assumptions.
* Fewer independent kinds of entities in a theory.
* A shorter description or smaller mathematical model.
* Fewer adjustable parameters.
* An explanation that is easier to test or understand.

These are not all equivalent. A formula with fewer parameters may fit the observations badly, and a shorter sentence may conceal many hidden assumptions.

== Where did the name come from? ==

The principle is associated with William of Ockham, a medieval philosopher. However, related appeals to simplicity existed before him, and modern popular versions do not necessarily reproduce his exact arguments or words. The familiar slogan about not multiplying entities beyond necessity should not be presented uncritically as a verified verbatim quotation from Ockham.<ref name="history">Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy, [https://iep.utm.edu/ockham/ ''William of Ockham''], historical context of parsimony and the name; Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, [https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ockham/ ''William of Ockham''].</ref>

== Does science use the idea? ==

Yes, researchers sometimes compare explanations by balancing their fit to data against extra complexity. Statistical model selection includes methods that penalize complexity, while Bayesian methods can evaluate how well models predict observed data before seeing those data.

These mathematical methods depend on specific assumptions. They are related to simplicity but are not interchangeable versions of one universal rule.

== When should a complex explanation win? ==

Whenever it gives a sufficiently better account of the evidence, makes accurate independent predictions, or represents important mechanisms missing from the simpler alternative. A correct complex mechanism should not be rejected just because it is hard to understand.

In practice, it is useful to ask which assumptions are unnecessary, what each explanation predicts, what new measurements would distinguish them, and how sensitive the conclusion is to the modeling choices.

== Why is it not a magic shortcut? ==

A simple story can be persuasive and wrong. Some events have multiple interacting causes, and some apparently short explanations hide many technical choices. Occam's razor cannot settle a scientific dispute without evidence or supply facts that were never measured.

It is related to [[Scientific model]], [[Epistemology]] and [[Reasoning]]. [[Abstraction/Simple|Abstraction]] concerns what information a representation preserves or omits; it is not automatically a recommendation to choose the shortest explanation.

